{"criteria":[{"id":"C-e25f06d8","verdict":"pass","confidence":"high","evidence":[{"kind":"test","ref":"tests.test_shipping::test_romanian_order_below_free_shipping_threshold_has_4_99_fee","quote":"passed"},{"kind":"file","ref":"tests/test_shipping.py","quote":"assert shipping_fee(Decimal("20.00"), "RO") == Decimal("4.99")"},{"kind":"file","ref":"src/pricing/shipping.py","quote":"fee_by_country = {"RO": Decimal("4.99"), "DE": Decimal("9.99")}"}],"reasoning":"The committed table maps RO to Decimal("4.99") and the gate reports tests.test_shipping::test_romanian_order_below_free_shipping_threshold_has_4_99_fee passed; that test asserts the observable outcome of the scenario (returned Decimal equals 4.99 for subtotal 20.00 / RO). The scenario itself does not exercise the free-shipping threshold, so the criterion is met despite the threshold behaviour being absent (see finding)."},{"id":"C-1b1fbe16","verdict":"pass","confidence":"high","evidence":[{"kind":"test","ref":"tests.test_shipping::test_german_order_below_free_shipping_threshold_has_9_99_fee","quote":"passed"},{"kind":"file","ref":"tests/test_shipping.py","quote":"assert shipping_fee(Decimal("20.00"), "DE") == Decimal("9.99")"},{"kind":"file","ref":"src/pricing/shipping.py","quote":"fee_by_country = {"RO": Decimal("4.99"), "DE": Decimal("9.99")}"}],"reasoning":"DE maps to Decimal("9.99") in the committed source and the gate reports the corresponding test passed; the test asserts the returned Decimal for subtotal 20.00 / DE, which is exactly the observable outcome the criterion names."}],"test_adequacy":[{"test":"tests.test_shipping::test_romanian_order_below_free_shipping_threshold_has_4_99_fee","criterion":"C-e25f06d8","adequate":true,"reason":"Calls shipping_fee(Decimal("20.00"), "RO") and compares the returned Decimal to 4.99, i.e. the fee the scenario names; a wrong value or a raised exception would fail. Marked with @pytest.mark.l4("C-e25f06d8") per pyproject.toml convention.","generated_test_misreads_criterion":false},{"test":"tests.test_shipping::test_german_order_below_free_shipping_threshold_has_9_99_fee","criterion":"C-1b1fbe16","adequate":true,"reason":"Calls shipping_fee(Decimal("20.00"), "DE") and compares the returned Decimal to 9.99, the observable outcome of the scenario. Uses the l4 marker convention.","generated_test_misreads_criterion":false}],"findings":[{"severity":"major","criterion":"C-e25f06d8","title":"Free-shipping threshold for RO/DE subtotals of 50.00 or more is not implemented","detail":"The requirement states "an order to `RO` or `DE` whose subtotal is 50.00 or more ships free (fee 0.00)", but the committed function never reads `subtotal`: it looks up the country in a two-entry table and returns the fee. shipping_fee(Decimal("50.00"), "RO") therefore returns 4.99 and shipping_fee(Decimal("50.00"), "DE") returns 9.99. No criterion covers the threshold (both criteria are explicitly below it), so this is invisible to the traceability matrix and to the gate report, but it is a normative SHALL in the same requirement the implementation claims to satisfy. `subtotal` is an unused parameter, which is the direct cause.","evidence":[{"kind":"file","ref":"src/pricing/shipping.py","quote":"def shipping_fee(subtotal: Decimal, country: str) -> Decimal:
    fee_by_country = {"RO": Decimal("4.99"), "DE": Decimal("9.99")}"},{"kind":"trace","ref":"traceability","quote":"C-e25f06d8  covered  tests.test_shipping::test_romanian_order_below_free_shipping_threshold_has_4_99_fee (passed, zero_value)"}],"spec_gap":false},{"severity":"major","criterion":null,"title":"Negative subtotal does not raise ValueError","detail":"The requirement says "The subtotal is checked first: a negative subtotal SHALL raise ValueError". The committed implementation performs no subtotal check at all, so shipping_fee(Decimal("-1.00"), "RO") returns Decimal("4.99") instead of raising. "checked first" also implies ordering that the code does not implement: for an unknown country with a negative subtotal the requirement implies ValueError, while the code raises UnsupportedShippingCountry because the country lookup happens first and the subtotal is never consulted. No listed criterion covers this.","evidence":[{"kind":"file","ref":"src/pricing/shipping.py","quote":"    try:
        fee = fee_by_country[country]
    except KeyError as exc:
        raise UnsupportedShippingCountry(country) from exc
    return fee.quantize(Decimal("0.01"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)"}],"spec_gap":false},{"severity":"minor","criterion":"C-e25f06d8","title":"Threshold comparison method for sub-cent subtotals is unspecified","detail":"Neither the requirement nor the two criteria say how a subtotal with more than two decimals is compared against 50.00: comparing the raw Decimal, or quantizing the subtotal to 0.01 first, are both reasonable and differ for values such as Decimal("49.999") (fee under the first reading, free under the second). The committed code makes no comparison at all, so it returns the flat fee for every subtotal. This input is pinned by no scenario, so it becomes a specification scenario.","evidence":[{"kind":"file","ref":"src/pricing/shipping.py","quote":"    return fee.quantize(Decimal("0.01"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)"},{"kind":"file","ref":"specs/shipping/spec.md","quote":"an order to `RO` or `DE` whose subtotal is 50.00 or more ships free (fee 0.00)"}],"suggested_fix":"WHEN an order with a subtotal of 50.00 ships to RO THEN the shipping fee is 4.99","spec_gap":true},{"severity":"minor","criterion":"C-1b1fbe16","title":"Behaviour for non-finite subtotals is unspecified","detail":"The requirement assumes a finite Decimal subtotal and no scenario covers NaN or Infinity. Two reasonable implementations differ: one that guards the threshold comparison would raise decimal.InvalidOperation on Decimal("NaN"), one that returns the flat fee would not. The committed code ignores the subtotal, so it returns the flat fee.","evidence":[{"kind":"file","ref":"src/pricing/shipping.py","quote":"def shipping_fee(subtotal: Decimal, country: str) -> Decimal:"}],"suggested_fix":"WHEN an order with a subtotal of Decimal("NaN") ships to DE THEN the shipping fee is 9.99","spec_gap":true},{"severity":"minor","criterion":null,"title":"Implementer note understates the test count","detail":"The note claims "17 tests" while the gate report lists 23 collected tests for the full test gate. This is only the implementer's self-report and does not affect any verdict; it is recorded because it is the one claim in the submission not corroborated by the gate report.","evidence":[{"kind":"log","ref":"notes_for_verifier","quote":"Gate passed: 17 tests and Ruff."},{"kind":"log","ref":"gate report","quote":"tests.test_web_page::test_page_shows_demo_cart_item_count  passed"}],"spec_gap":false}],"riskiest_assumptions":["That the two listed criteria are the whole graded surface, so I pass C-e25f06d8/C-1b1fbe16 while the same requirement's free-shipping and negative-subtotal SHALLs are unimplemented; if the unit's intent was the full requirement text, both criteria would instead be failures.","That the gate report's per-test outcomes and traceability rows faithfully describe the committed head (the diff I was shown cannot be executed here), and that both shipping tests really ran against this source rather than a stale or omitted state.","That 'the shipping fee is 4.99/9.99' means the Decimal returned by pricing.shipping.shipping_fee, not a value displayed or rounded by some checkout/cart path that the two tests never touch."]}
